1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,687
Three sentences every DevOps engineer hears.

2
00:00:03,687 --> 00:00:04,773
Java not found.

3
00:00:04,773 --> 00:00:07,163
Class file has the wrong version.

4
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:08,756
It works on my laptop.

5
00:00:09,306 --> 00:00:13,412
Behind all three sits one confusion: what is Java,

6
00:00:13,412 --> 00:00:13,987
really?

7
00:00:13,987 --> 00:00:14,561
Source,

8
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:15,300
bytecode,

9
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:15,793
J D K,

10
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:16,286
J R E,

11
00:00:16,286 --> 00:00:16,779
J V M.

12
00:00:16,779 --> 00:00:19,653
Five words people mix up every day.

13
00:00:20,203 --> 00:00:24,939
Today you separate them, one at a time, and you prove each one on a real machine.

14
00:00:25,489 --> 00:00:29,499
By the end, when a Java build fails, you will know which part is missing.

15
00:00:31,549 --> 00:00:36,498
Here is the whole journey of a Java program, in five steps. Follow the parcel.

16
00:00:37,048 --> 00:00:37,316
One.

17
00:00:37,316 --> 00:00:37,783
Source.

18
00:00:37,783 --> 00:00:39,988
A plain text file that you write,

19
00:00:39,988 --> 00:00:41,258
ending in dot java.

20
00:00:41,258 --> 00:00:42,527
Humans can read it.

21
00:00:42,527 --> 00:00:44,131
Computers cannot run it.

22
00:00:44,681 --> 00:00:44,969
Two.

23
00:00:44,969 --> 00:00:45,907
The compiler,

24
00:00:45,907 --> 00:00:46,916
called java C.

25
00:00:46,916 --> 00:00:49,655
It reads the source and translates it.

26
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:51,241
It lives in the J D K,

27
00:00:51,241 --> 00:00:53,044
the Java Development Kit.

28
00:00:53,594 --> 00:00:54,008
Three.

29
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:54,631
Bytecode.

30
00:00:54,631 --> 00:00:56,497
A file ending in dot class.

31
00:00:56,497 --> 00:00:58,087
It is not machine code.

32
00:00:58,087 --> 00:01:01,475
It is an instruction list for a made-up computer,

33
00:01:01,475 --> 00:01:04,516
so it is the same on every operating system.

34
00:01:05,066 --> 00:01:05,448
Four.

35
00:01:05,448 --> 00:01:06,210
The J V M,

36
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,116
the Java Virtual Machine.

37
00:01:08,116 --> 00:01:09,793
That made-up computer,

38
00:01:09,793 --> 00:01:11,166
as a real program.

39
00:01:11,166 --> 00:01:13,758
It reads the bytecode and runs it.

40
00:01:13,758 --> 00:01:15,741
It ships inside the J R E,

41
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:17,952
the Java Runtime Environment.

42
00:01:18,502 --> 00:01:18,856
Five.

43
00:01:18,856 --> 00:01:19,708
The process.

44
00:01:19,708 --> 00:01:21,056
When you type java,

45
00:01:21,056 --> 00:01:22,262
and a class name,

46
00:01:22,262 --> 00:01:25,739
the operating system starts a process named java,

47
00:01:25,739 --> 00:01:27,867
and the J V M lives inside it.

48
00:01:28,417 --> 00:01:35,201
Remember the nesting. The J D K contains the J R E. The J R E contains the J V M.

49
00:01:35,751 --> 00:01:40,700
So: to build, you need the J D K. To only run, the J R E is enough.

50
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:48,467
Time to prove it, on a real Ubuntu machine. Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

51
00:01:49,017 --> 00:01:52,068
First, the runner. Type: java, dash version.

52
00:01:52,618 --> 00:01:58,975
Open J D K, version twenty-one. This is the runtime: the J V M, plus its libraries.

53
00:01:59,525 --> 00:02:02,896
Now the compiler. Type: java C, dash version.

54
00:02:03,446 --> 00:02:04,601
Java C twenty-one.

55
00:02:04,601 --> 00:02:06,589
If this said command not found,

56
00:02:06,589 --> 00:02:09,668
you would have a runtime but no development kit.

57
00:02:09,668 --> 00:02:12,939
That is the most common surprise on a fresh server.

58
00:02:13,489 --> 00:02:16,631
What does the runtime package install?

59
00:02:16,631 --> 00:02:17,788
Type: D P K G,

60
00:02:17,788 --> 00:02:19,028
dash capital L,

61
00:02:19,028 --> 00:02:19,937
open J D K,

62
00:02:19,937 --> 00:02:20,847
twenty-one,

63
00:02:20,847 --> 00:02:21,177
jre,

64
00:02:21,177 --> 00:02:21,921
headless,

65
00:02:21,921 --> 00:02:22,335
pipe,

66
00:02:22,335 --> 00:02:22,748
grep,

67
00:02:22,748 --> 00:02:24,071
slash bin slash.

68
00:02:24,621 --> 00:02:31,213
Only four tools: java, J package, key tool, and R M I registry. Notice: no compiler.

69
00:02:31,763 --> 00:02:33,748
And the development kit?

70
00:02:33,748 --> 00:02:35,072
Count its tools.

71
00:02:35,072 --> 00:02:36,230
Type: D P K G,

72
00:02:36,230 --> 00:02:37,471
dash capital L,

73
00:02:37,471 --> 00:02:38,381
open J D K,

74
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:39,291
twenty-one,

75
00:02:39,291 --> 00:02:39,787
J D K,

76
00:02:39,787 --> 00:02:40,531
headless,

77
00:02:40,531 --> 00:02:40,945
pipe,

78
00:02:40,945 --> 00:02:41,359
grep,

79
00:02:41,359 --> 00:02:41,938
dash C,

80
00:02:41,938 --> 00:02:43,261
slash bin slash.

81
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:45,426
Twenty-four tools.

82
00:02:45,426 --> 00:02:46,054
Java C,

83
00:02:46,054 --> 00:02:46,413
jar,

84
00:02:46,413 --> 00:02:46,952
J P S,

85
00:02:46,952 --> 00:02:48,298
J cmd and more.

86
00:02:48,298 --> 00:02:50,541
The J D K is the runtime,

87
00:02:50,541 --> 00:02:52,963
plus the builder's toolbox.

88
00:02:55,013 --> 00:02:59,344
Now step one to three, for real. Go to the shop. Type: CD, shop.

89
00:02:59,894 --> 00:03:03,200
Here is the source. Type: cat, Hello dot java.

90
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:09,596
A class named Hello, and one method, main. Two lines print text. This is only text so far.

91
00:03:10,146 --> 00:03:13,388
Compile it. Type: java C, Hello dot java.

92
00:03:13,938 --> 00:03:16,925
No output. In Unix, silence means success.

93
00:03:17,475 --> 00:03:19,182
What appeared? Type: L S.

94
00:03:19,732 --> 00:03:24,852
A new file: Hello dot class. Same name, new extension. That is the bytecode.

95
00:03:25,402 --> 00:03:27,047
Is it really not text?

96
00:03:27,047 --> 00:03:29,290
Look at the first eight bytes.

97
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:30,188
Type: X X D,

98
00:03:30,188 --> 00:03:30,711
dash L,

99
00:03:30,711 --> 00:03:31,160
eight,

100
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:32,356
Hello dot class.

101
00:03:32,906 --> 00:03:33,600
Cafe babe.

102
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:37,140
Every Java class file starts with these four bytes.

103
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:37,487
Then,

104
00:03:37,487 --> 00:03:38,459
hex forty-one,

105
00:03:38,459 --> 00:03:42,762
which is sixty-five: the bytecode version for Java twenty-one.

106
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:45,595
Now run it. Type: java, Hello.

107
00:03:46,145 --> 00:03:51,820
Hello from Cloud Cart, on Java twenty-one. The J V M read the bytecode and ran it.

108
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:58,670
Let us look inside the bytecode. First go to the shop. Type: CD, shop.

109
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:04,383
Compile again, so the class file exists. Type: java C, Hello dot java.

110
00:04:04,933 --> 00:04:05,228
Now,

111
00:04:05,228 --> 00:04:06,483
the disassembler.

112
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:07,443
Type: java P,

113
00:04:07,443 --> 00:04:07,960
dash C,

114
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:08,403
Hello,

115
00:04:08,403 --> 00:04:10,322
then pipe to cut and head,

116
00:04:10,322 --> 00:04:12,463
only to keep the screen tidy.

117
00:04:13,013 --> 00:04:14,149
Not machine code.

118
00:04:14,149 --> 00:04:17,424
Small steps for the made-up computer: get static,

119
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:18,494
load a constant,

120
00:04:18,494 --> 00:04:19,496
invoke virtual.

121
00:04:19,496 --> 00:04:21,568
That last one calls print line.

122
00:04:22,118 --> 00:04:22,831
Any J V M,

123
00:04:22,831 --> 00:04:23,829
on any system,

124
00:04:23,829 --> 00:04:25,254
can run these steps.

125
00:04:25,254 --> 00:04:28,390
That is why one class file works everywhere.

126
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:34,067
Last: the process. Go to the shop. Type: CD, shop.

127
00:04:34,617 --> 00:04:39,203
Compile the second program. Type: java C, Shop App dot java.

128
00:04:39,753 --> 00:04:45,684
Start it in the background. Type: java, Shop App, redirect to app dot log, and ampersand.

129
00:04:47,734 --> 00:04:53,238
A process now runs. Ask the Java tool for the list. Type: J P S, dash L.

130
00:04:53,788 --> 00:05:00,017
Shop App, with its process I D. J P S itself shows up too, because it is a Java program.

131
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:07,095
Now ask the operating system. Type: P S, dash C, java, dash O, pid, rss, args.

132
00:05:07,645 --> 00:05:08,262
Same I D.

133
00:05:08,262 --> 00:05:12,238
The operating system sees an ordinary process called java.

134
00:05:12,238 --> 00:05:14,569
The memory column is in kilobytes.

135
00:05:14,569 --> 00:05:16,968
The class name is only an argument.

136
00:05:17,518 --> 00:05:19,715
Stop it. Type: kill, percent one.

137
00:05:20,265 --> 00:05:22,996
Check again. Type: J P S, dash L.

138
00:05:23,546 --> 00:05:23,856
Gone.

139
00:05:23,856 --> 00:05:24,972
The process ended,

140
00:05:24,972 --> 00:05:27,142
and the bytecode file is untouched.

141
00:05:27,142 --> 00:05:30,181
The program and the process are different things.

142
00:05:32,231 --> 00:05:36,199
Now use it. Three real error messages, and what each one tells you.

143
00:05:36,749 --> 00:05:42,594
java, command not found. No runtime at all. Install a J R E or a J D K.

144
00:05:43,144 --> 00:05:43,660
java C,

145
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:44,987
command not found.

146
00:05:44,987 --> 00:05:46,388
You have a runtime,

147
00:05:46,388 --> 00:05:48,452
but not the development kit.

148
00:05:48,452 --> 00:05:49,779
Install the J D K.

149
00:05:50,329 --> 00:05:52,847
Unsupported class file major version.

150
00:05:52,847 --> 00:05:56,250
The bytecode is newer than the J V M that runs it.

151
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:58,360
Compile with the right version,

152
00:05:58,360 --> 00:05:59,993
or run with a newer one.

153
00:06:00,543 --> 00:06:04,767
Each message points at exactly one part of the journey. That is the skill.

154
00:06:06,817 --> 00:06:07,733
Your lab task.

155
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:11,003
Lab seventy-six builds two small programs for you.

156
00:06:11,003 --> 00:06:12,769
Lab check judges your work.

157
00:06:13,319 --> 00:06:15,535
It lives at git hub dot com,

158
00:06:15,535 --> 00:06:17,117
slash it dash waves,

159
00:06:17,117 --> 00:06:18,937
slash dev ops dash A I,

160
00:06:18,937 --> 00:06:19,807
in academy,

161
00:06:19,807 --> 00:06:20,124
lab,

162
00:06:20,124 --> 00:06:22,023
lab seventy-six dot S H.

163
00:06:22,573 --> 00:06:26,050
One: save both version outputs into versions dot text.

164
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,310
Two: compile both programs, and run Hello.

165
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:33,081
Three: save the disassembly into byte code dot text.

166
00:06:33,631 --> 00:06:36,335
Four: start Shop App in the background,

167
00:06:36,335 --> 00:06:37,375
and save J P S,

168
00:06:37,375 --> 00:06:37,860
dash L,

169
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,386
into process dot text.

170
00:06:39,386 --> 00:06:40,564
Leave it running.

171
00:06:41,114 --> 00:06:46,532
Five: write three lines in answers dot text: java C comes with the J D K,

172
00:06:46,532 --> 00:06:48,759
byte code is a dot class file,

173
00:06:48,759 --> 00:06:51,653
and the program runs as a java process.

174
00:06:52,203 --> 00:06:52,754
Install,

175
00:06:52,754 --> 00:06:53,581
then lab up,

176
00:06:53,581 --> 00:06:54,270
lab check,

177
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:55,166
and lab down,

178
00:06:55,166 --> 00:06:57,646
the same way as in the last lessons.

179
00:06:57,646 --> 00:07:00,608
The shortcut file is shortcuts seventy-six.

180
00:07:02,658 --> 00:07:04,727
Pass or fail. You need all six.

181
00:07:05,277 --> 00:07:07,091
Five pass lines in lab check.

182
00:07:07,641 --> 00:07:12,569
You can name the five parts: source, java C, bytecode, J V M, process.

183
00:07:13,119 --> 00:07:17,705
You can say which one is in the J D K and which one is in the J R E.

184
00:07:18,255 --> 00:07:20,879
You can show the cafe babe bytes of a class file.

185
00:07:21,429 --> 00:07:23,648
You can show a process I D in two ways.

186
00:07:24,198 --> 00:07:26,673
lab down, and nothing java left running.

187
00:07:27,223 --> 00:07:28,524
To pass, all six.

188
00:07:30,574 --> 00:07:31,290
Your turn,

189
00:07:31,290 --> 00:07:33,795
in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

190
00:07:33,795 --> 00:07:35,370
Install a J D K first,

191
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:37,017
if you do not have one.

192
00:07:37,567 --> 00:07:39,657
One: lab up, and read the task file.

193
00:07:40,207 --> 00:07:42,085
Two: versions, compile, run.

194
00:07:42,635 --> 00:07:44,149
Three: the disassembly.

195
00:07:44,699 --> 00:07:46,769
Four: start the app, list the process.

196
00:07:47,319 --> 00:07:49,601
Five: write your answers, and lab check.

197
00:07:50,151 --> 00:07:52,733
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

198
00:07:53,283 --> 00:07:55,117
Pause the video, and begin now.

199
00:08:10,667 --> 00:08:11,542
Quick check.

200
00:08:12,092 --> 00:08:14,950
What turns source into bytecode, and which kit has it?

201
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:17,911
What do the first four bytes of a class file say?

202
00:08:18,461 --> 00:08:21,533
Which part runs the bytecode, and what package ships it?

203
00:08:22,083 --> 00:08:24,472
Is the program the same thing as the process?

204
00:08:25,022 --> 00:08:28,670
